# ADR-001-A: Backend Language & Runtime

**Status:** Accepted

## Context

Following architecture review, the team elected to adopt a hybrid backend architecture that combines enterprise-grade Java services with Node.js services optimized for integration and real-time workloads.

## Decision

Tablé will use a polyglot backend architecture consisting of:

- Spring Boot (Java 21)
- Node.js (TypeScript)
- Python FastAPI (ML workloads only)

### Service Allocation

| Service | Technology |
|----------|------------|
| API Gateway | Node.js (NestJS) |
| Reservations | Spring Boot |
| Marketplace | Spring Boot |
| Realtime | Node.js |
| Notifications | Node.js |
| User Profile | Spring Boot |
| ML Pipeline | Python FastAPI |

## Consequences

- OpenAPI specifications become the authoritative service contract.
- GitHub Actions must support Java, Node.js, and Python build pipelines.
- Integration testing becomes mandatory across language boundaries.

---

# ADR-001-B: Microservices Decomposition Strategy

**Status:** Accepted

## Decision

The platform will maintain seven independently deployable services:

| Service | Domain |
|----------|----------|
| api-gateway | Routing, authentication, rate limiting |
| reservations | Reservation workflows |
| marketplace | Deal marketplace |
| realtime | WebSocket communication |
| notifications | Push notification delivery |
| user-profile | Preferences and personalization |
| ml-pipeline | Recommendation and prediction engine |

## Consequences

- Independent deployment capability.
- Service-level ownership.
- Distributed tracing required across all services.

---

# ADR-001-C: ML Pipeline Exception

**Status:** Accepted

## Decision

The ML Pipeline remains the only approved Python service.

### Responsibilities

- Restaurant recommendation ranking
- User-to-deal matching
- Demand prediction
- Busyness forecasting
- Confidence scoring

### Constraints

- No transactional ownership.
- No authentication handling.
- No direct business workflow orchestration.
- Must remain stateless.

---

# ADR-001-D: Cloud Deployment Strategy

**Status:** Accepted

## Context

Cloud deployment architecture is owned by the Integration Lead.

## Decision

The deployment strategy will be:

### Primary Platform

Google Cloud Platform (GCP)

### Secondary Platform

Amazon Web Services (AWS)

AWS remains an approved fallback deployment option.

Infrastructure provisioning, deployment governance, CI/CD pipelines, and environment management are owned by the Integration Lead.

---

## Target GCP Architecture

| Layer | Technology |
|---------|---------|
| Containers | Cloud Run |
| API Gateway | Google API Gateway |
| Database | Cloud SQL (PostgreSQL) |
| Cache | Memorystore (Redis) |
| Storage | Google Cloud Storage |
| Secrets | Secret Manager |
| Monitoring | Cloud Operations Suite |
| Logging | Cloud Logging |
| CI/CD | GitHub Actions |
| ML Storage | GCS Buckets |

---

## Environment Strategy

| Environment | Purpose |
|-------------|---------|
| Development | Local Docker |
| Staging | Auto-deploy from develop |
| Production | Release deployment only |

---

## CI/CD Strategy

GitHub Actions is the standard deployment platform.

### Pipeline

```text
GitHub Actions
├── lint
├── test
├── build
├── security-scan
├── api-contract-validation
├── container-build
├── terraform-plan
├── terraform-apply
└── deployment
````

## Consequences

* Terraform remains the preferred IaC framework.
* Automatic deployment to staging on merge to develop.
* Production deployments require release approval.
* Infrastructure reviews require Integration Lead approval.

